#speculation(title: [H4 Receptor as the Untargeted Fourth Histamine Arm])[
The histamine H4 receptor (H4R) is a fourth receptor arm expressed on immune cells — mast cells, basophils, eosinophils, T cells, and dendritic cells — where it drives chemotaxis and trafficking that the H1 receptor does not cover @Gibbs2012H4MastBasophil @Walter2011H4Inflammation. H4R mediates pruritus and Th2 inflammation independently of H1, so combined H1+H4 blockade outperforms H1 blockade alone in atopic dermatitis models @Cowden2010H4Pruritus @Ohsawa2014H1H4AtopicDerm. A standard H1/H2/CysLT1 stack therefore leaves this arm pharmacologically untouched @Mehta2020H4Enigmatic. H4R antagonists remain research-stage: izuforant has reached phase I pharmacokinetic/safety testing only, with no approved H4R antagonist available @Jin2024IzuforantH4R. All supporting evidence is general, allergic, or preclinical — no ME/CFS or MCAS cohort has been studied. (Certainty: 0.35)

_Falsifiable prediction:_ In MCAS patients with incomplete symptom control on maximal H1/H2/CysLT1 blockade, an H4R antagonist produces additional symptom reduction that H1/H2/CysLT1 blockade alone cannot achieve (measured by pruritus or flare severity, not histamine release).

*Consequence:* If H4R blockade adds benefit over the H1/H2/CysLT1 stack, residual MCAS symptoms in treated patients would not indicate treatment failure of the stack but an unaddressed fourth arm. This is a mechanistic research claim, not a treatment recommendation — no H4R antagonist is approved for any indication.
] <spec:h4-receptor-untargeted-arm>
